There are basically two ways forward: (1) flat tax rate and (2) slightly progressive but no tax exps of any kind (completely flat).  

If you make it progressive than you get around the concern that you are taxing firms that really cannot afford this. 
But you come up against another concern: that firms may be broken up into when they are in fact one entity. 


The standard NIPA measure of corporate profits is calculated after depreciation, but it uses an economic, current-cost measure rather than tax or book depreciation. In the headline series—corporate profits with inventory valuation and capital consumption adjustments (IVA and CCAdj)—the BEA replaces the depreciation reported on tax returns with an estimate of the actual economic wear-and-tear on capital at current replacement cost. This adjustment ensures that profits reflect income from current production rather than accounting conventions or accelerated tax depreciation rules. By contrast, the series for corporate profits before tax without IVA and CCAdj uses the depreciation recorded in firms’ tax or book accounts. In both cases profits are measured after depreciation; the difference is whether the depreciation reflects tax accounting or a standardized economic concept.
